WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.830
Nature spent three hundred million years perfecting the beetle.

2
00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:11.810
I had a weekend, a blank viewport, and a slightly unhealthy

3
00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:12.970
obsession with brass.

4
00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.580
It started with a simple question:

5
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.740
what if an insect wasn't grown, but machined?

6
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.660
A heavy, low-slung scarab dressed in British racing green,

7
00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.820
champagne brass, and exposed industrial steel.

8
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:26.800
The catch?

9
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.440
A concept render only has to look convincing from one angle.

10
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.380
A 3D machine has to make sense from all of them.

11
00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:36.090
Everything starts with the undercarriage.

12
00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.770
Instead of chitin and tissue, we’re laying down tubular steel

13
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.710
rails and heavy modular housings.

14
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.710
Every bolt and bracket locks in six independent hip actuators.

15
00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:49.590
In mechanical design, six hips means six separate points of

16
00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:52.730
failure—so the tolerances here must be unforgiving.

17
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.680
With radial symmetry, you only build one limb—so you make it

18
00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:02.000
count. Precision rotary bearings, a machined collar, tapered armor

19
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.080
plating, and a high-grip rubber foot.

20
00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:07.240
Nail the geometry once, and you’ve built a stance.

21
00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.719
Miss a bevel by a millimeter, and you’ve just multiplied your

22
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:11.880
mistake by six.

23
00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:17.030
Locked into the chassis, three pairs of legs splay wide and low,

24
00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.550
dropping the center of gravity right to the floor.

25
00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:21.350
It isn’t built to be delicate.

26
00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.650
It’s built to plant six contact points into the ground like an

27
00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:26.930
armored tank—and refuse to budge.

28
00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:29.450
Now, the engine block.

29
00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.390
A heavy central pressure drum drops into the bay, flanked by twin

30
00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.410
pneumatic pistons and high-pressure lines.

31
00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:39.190
Is it held down by way more bolts than physics actually requires?

32
00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:43.910
Absolutely. Because 'strictly necessary' never made anyone stare

33
00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:44.510
at a machine.

34
00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.740
Right now, it’s still just a walking engine.

35
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.660
Giving it a sweeping cowl, twin glass optics, and segmented

36
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.100
antennae changes the psychology completely.

37
00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.740
The moment the eyes slot into place, your brain stops seeing

38
00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:58.260
hardware...

39
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.200
and starts seeing a creature.

40
00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:08.010
Up to this point, we've been living in raw grey clay—pure form and

41
00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:08.470
shadow.

42
00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:12.350
Now, watch what happens when light hits metallic emerald flake,

43
00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:15.110
brushed champagne brass, and satin graphite.

44
00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:18.290
That is why we spend hours tweaking shaders.

45
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.620
Finally, the carapace.

46
00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.260
Two sweeping wing covers trimmed in brass drop over the core,

47
00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.340
locking onto heavy shoulder hinges.

48
00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.080
A single raised seam runs down the spine,

49
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.380
sealing away all that chaotic machinery underneath behind one

50
00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.480
clean, uninterrupted silhouette.

51
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:41.240
Build complete.

52
00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.060
Let's bring the core online.

53
00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:44.960
Optics hot.

54
00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:47.280
Pressure stabilized.

55
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.160
Wing actuators clear for full deployment.

56
00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.240
And just like that, it’s ready to move.

57
00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.220
Well, mostly.

58
00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.160
Turns out modeling six legs is one thing;

59
00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.620
rigging a six-legged walk cycle without it ice-skating out of the

60
00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:12.820
studio is another.

61
00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.000
Follow for more builds like this.

62
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.700
The model is available to download on GitHub.
